# InterMix log rotation — Rails production.log + the ActivityPub worker logs.
#
# Install on staging and production as /etc/logrotate.d/intermix (root, 0644):
#
#   scp config/logrotate.example root@<host>:/etc/logrotate.d/intermix
#   ssh root@<host> 'chmod 644 /etc/logrotate.d/intermix && \
#                    logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/intermix'   # dry run first
#
# Both servers already run logrotate daily on their own (production via
# /etc/cron.daily/logrotate, staging via the logrotate.timer systemd unit), so
# dropping the file in is the whole install — nothing to schedule.
#
# WHY copytruncate: Passenger and the two cron workers hold this file handle
# open for the life of the process. Renaming the file (logrotate's default)
# would leave them appending to an unlinked inode — the rotated archive would
# keep growing invisibly and the fresh production.log would stay empty until
# the next app restart. copytruncate copies then truncates in place, so the
# open handles keep working. Its known cost is a race: log lines written
# between the copy and the truncate are lost. That is inherent to the
# approach and acceptable for a request log.
#
# WHY su ploy ploy: the log directory belongs to ploy, not root. logrotate
# 3.21 (staging) refuses to rotate files in a directory it does not own
# unless told which user to act as. Production runs 3.11, which is laxer,
# but the directive is honoured there too — keep both servers identical.
#
# NOTE the *.log glob deliberately does not match
# production.log.archive-2020-03-09_2026-07-25.gz on production — that is a
# permanent one-off archive of the first 5 years of logs (2 GB, 145 MB
# gzipped, byte-verified before the live file was truncated on 2026-07-25).
# It is outside the rotation pool on purpose, so `rotate 26` can never age it
# out. Do not rename it to something ending in .log.

/home/apps/intermix/shared/log/*.log {
    weekly
    rotate 26
    maxsize 200M
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    copytruncate
    su ploy ploy
}
